      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14130082/assets/7d2474d5fd25cbb23c534e4d8f67726177988a49f9c0e271b91b0ff677dabd41.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $\\frac{2}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we need to multiply by $\\frac{1}{4}$, we split the whole into $4$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14130082/assets/416c66bc1591d9215bd5e74c975f6e17992ae0d3456262091d48910b9d89abc9.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\frac{2}{12}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{2}{12}.\n$$",
